    statement: "Use N finite-horizon, binary-reward tasks with a shared bounded-feature softmax policy and a fixed evaluation mixture. The curriculum chooses the next task; task-to-task gradient influence is assumed to have rank at most r and bounded variation over training, with probes charged to the budget. Determine the excess cost of learning difficulty and transfer online relative to an equally resourced oracle that knows this influence, including negative transfer and failures of static difficulty ranking.",
    assumptions: ["Evaluate on a fixed target distribution; selecting only easy training tasks must not count as progress.", "Specify the task generator and transfer structure, such as a bounded-rank influence model, and the feedback available to the curriculum.", "Include the cost of difficulty probes and any coverage floor or importance correction. State how negative transfer enters the guarantee."],
    motivation: "Capability-adaptive generators maintain reward variation, but initial success rates need not predict future learning or cross-task benefit.",
