    aliases: ["Reliable RL scaling extrapolation under adaptive pilot selection", "Scaling-law uncertainty", "Sigmoid versus power-law identifiability"], area: "learning_theory", secondary: ["learning_dynamics"], tags: ["scaling_laws", "experimental_design", "uncertainty"], framework: true,
    statement: "For a finite set of RL recipes, model expected success as a logistic curve in log compute with bounded intercept, slope and ceiling, plus a discrepancy bounded by eta on a stated pilot-and-extrapolation interval. Observations have conditionally sub-Gaussian noise and are selected adaptively. Determine minimax prediction intervals and pilot designs that distinguish slope/efficiency changes from ceiling changes; quantify how the interval width grows with extrapolation distance and discrepancy, including indistinguishable-curve lower bounds.",
    assumptions: ["Declare the observable metric and fitted/structural curve family; reward, error rate, negative log-success and Elo-like scores are not interchangeable.", "Include training-seed variation and reuse of evaluation tasks. A finite-range fit does not identify arbitrary behavior at infinite compute.", "Any extrapolation guarantee must name a bounded range and assumptions excluding indistinguishable alternatives; absent those assumptions, seek an impossibility bound."],
    motivation: "A compute-allocation recipe can be misleading if its uncertainty omits curve-family ambiguity or the adaptive way pilots were chosen.",
