    aliases: ["Certifying backdoor absence", "White-box backdoor detection limits", "Hidden failure-mode auditing"], area: "alignment", secondary: ["verification_and_search"], tags: ["ai_safety", "backdoors", "certification", "computational_hardness"], framework: true,
    statement: "For depth-L ReLU classifiers with rational weights of at most b bits and bounded layer norms on [0,1]^d, allow triggers that replace at most k input coordinates by fixed values. Given a trusted clean-data sampler and labels, determine the sample and runtime needed to certify that no allowed trigger causes a wrong target-class prediction on more than an epsilon fraction of clean inputs, or to produce a violating trigger. Compare weight access with query-only auditing and expose dependence on k, L and b.",
    assumptions: ["Fix a finite-precision network class, an explicit unsafe-output predicate, a deployment distribution and a trigger class; distinguish worst-case absence from small distributional failure probability.", "Specify the auditor's access and computational budget. Positive results must exclude the known indistinguishable constructions through a substantive assumption, not by assuming detectable backdoors.", "Charge both inspection and testing; any minimum trigger mass, reference-model guarantee or provenance trust must be explicit."],
